\title{A Transfer-Operator Framework for Fibonacci Correlations: Cyclicity from
Finite Noncommutative State Spaces and Primitive Prime Divisors}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-02}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop an operator-theoretic framework in which the Fibonacci recurrence is
generated by a single non-commuting, reversible transfer operator, and use it to
derive two structural results together with a bridge between them. First, the
reduction of the transfer operator modulo any integer $m \ge 1$ is a unit of a
\emph{finite} noncommutative monoid; finiteness plus reversibility forces its
powers to cycle, and feeding this back through the operator-power representation
yields, with no residue chasing, the periodicity of the Fibonacci sequence modulo
$m$ (the classical Pisano periods), with the period identified as the
multiplicative order of the transfer unit in the general linear group over the
residue ring. Second, we give an elementary proof of the prime case of
Carmichael's primitive-divisor theorem: for every prime index $n \ge 13$ the
Fibonacci number $F_n$ has a primitive prime divisor. Finally we combine the two:
for a prime index $n \ge 13$ there is a prime born exactly at index $n$ whose
subsequent appearances recur periodically on the schedule set by the operator's
order. We situate these results within the program of entanglement-inspired
algorithmic complexity in noncommutative spaces, where simulating distant
correlations reduces to collapsing high powers of the transfer operator---a task
equivalent to order-finding---and where the first-appearance index of a primitive
divisor behaves like a one-way function.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The Fibonacci sequence $F_0 = 0$, $F_1 = 1$, $F_{n+2} = F_{n+1} + F_n$ is the
prototypical linear recurrence. Two of its classical structural properties---
periodicity modulo an integer (the Pisano periods) and the existence of primitive
prime divisors (Carmichael's theorem)---are usually treated by independent
techniques. We place both inside one operator-theoretic picture motivated by an
analogy with quantum correlations.

The organizing object is the \emph{transfer operator}
\[
M = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix},
\]
whose repeated application propagates the recurrence and whose powers encode all
Fibonacci correlations. Two features drive everything: \textbf{reversibility}
($\det M = -1$ is a unit, so $M$ is invertible over any coefficient ring), and
\textbf{noncommutativity} (matrix multiplication does not commute). Reducing
modulo $m$ makes the ambient algebra a \emph{finite} noncommutative monoid, and a
unit of a finite monoid has finite multiplicative order---the discrete analogue of
recurrence of reversible dynamics on a finite-dimensional Hilbert space.

\section{The transfer operator and its power representation}

\begin{definition}[Fibonacci numbers]
$F_0 = 0$, $F_1 = 1$, and $F_{n+2} = F_{n+1} + F_n$ for $n \ge 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Transfer operator]
Over a commutative ring $R$ with unit,
$M = \left(\begin{smallmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{smallmatrix}\right) \in
\Mat_{2\times 2}(R)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Primitive prime divisor]
A prime $p$ is a \emph{primitive prime divisor} of $F_n$ if $p \mid F_n$ and
$p \nmid F_k$ for every $k$ with $0 < k < n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Pisano period]
For $m \ge 1$, a \emph{period} of the sequence modulo $m$ is a positive integer
$p$ with $F_{n+p} \equiv F_n \pmod m$ for all $n$; the least such $p$ is the
Pisano period $\pi(m)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Power representation]\label{lem:power}
Indexing rows and columns by $\{0,1\}$, for all $k \ge 0$
\[
M^{k+1} = \begin{pmatrix} F_{k+2} & F_{k+1} \\ F_{k+1} & F_{k} \end{pmatrix},
\qquad \text{so} \qquad \big(M^{k+1}\big)_{1,1} = F_k.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $k$. The base case $M^1 = M$ matches
$\left(\begin{smallmatrix} F_2 & F_1 \\ F_1 & F_0 \end{smallmatrix}\right)$. The
step is $M^{k+2} = M\cdot M^{k+1}$ together with $F_{k+2} = F_{k+1} + F_k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Cassini's identity from a multiplicative invariant]
Taking determinants in Lemma~\ref{lem:power} and using
$\det(M^{k+1}) = (-1)^{k+1}$ gives $F_{k+2}F_k - F_{k+1}^2 = (-1)^{k+1}$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Cyclicity from a finite noncommutative state space}

\begin{theorem}[Pisano periodicity from a finite noncommutative group]\label{thm:period}
For every modulus $m \ge 1$ there exists a period $p > 0$ with
$F_{n+p} \equiv F_n \pmod m$ for all $n \ge 0$. Moreover $p$ can be taken to be
the multiplicative order of the reduced transfer unit $M \in \GL_2(\Z/m\Z)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Work over $R = \Z/m\Z$, finite and nonzero since $m \ge 1$.

\emph{Step 1: $M$ is a unit.} $\det M = -1$ is a unit of $R$; a matrix over a
commutative ring is invertible iff its determinant is a unit, so $M$ is a unit of
$\Mat_{2\times2}(R)$. Write $M = u \in \GL_2(R)$.

\emph{Step 2: finite order.} $\GL_2(R)$ is finite, so $p := \ord(u) > 0$ and
$u^p = 1$, whence $M^p = I$.

\emph{Step 3: transport.} For any $n$,
$M^{(n+p)+1} = M^{(n+1)+p} = M^{n+1} M^p = M^{n+1}$, and reading the $(1,1)$ entry
via Lemma~\ref{lem:power} gives $\overline{F_{n+p}} = \overline{F_n}$ in $R$, i.e.
$F_{n+p} \equiv F_n \pmod m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
For $m = 0$, $\Z/0\Z \cong \Z$ is infinite and $M$ has infinite order; finiteness
of the state space is exactly what is used. Computing least periods for
$m = 1,\dots,10$ yields $1,3,8,6,20,24,16,12,24,60$, the orders of $M$ in the
respective groups $\GL_2(\Z/m\Z)$.
\end{remark}

\section{Primitive prime divisors: the prime case}

\begin{lemma}[Strong divisibility]\label{lem:gcd}
For all $a,b \ge 0$, $\gcd(F_a, F_b) = F_{\gcd(a,b)}$. In particular, a prime
dividing both $F_a$ and $F_b$ divides $F_{\gcd(a,b)}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The addition formula $F_{a+b} = F_{a+1}F_b + F_a F_{b-1}$ gives
$\gcd(F_{a+b},F_b) = \gcd(F_a,F_b)$, mirroring one Euclidean step on the indices;
iterating carries $\gcd(F_a,F_b)$ to $\gcd(F_{\gcd(a,b)},F_0) = F_{\gcd(a,b)}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Carmichael, prime case]\label{thm:prim}
For every prime $n \ge 13$, $F_n$ has a primitive prime divisor.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $n \ge 3$ and the Fibonacci numbers are monotone from index $3$ with
$F_3 = 2 > 1$, we have $F_n > 1$, so $F_n$ has a prime divisor $p$. Suppose
$p \mid F_k$ for some $0 < k < n$. Then $p \mid \gcd(F_n, F_k) = F_{\gcd(n,k)}$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:gcd}. As $\gcd(n,k)$ divides the prime $n$ and satisfies
$\gcd(n,k) \le k < n$, we get $\gcd(n,k) = 1$, so $p \mid F_1 = 1$, a
contradiction. Hence $p$ is primitive.
\end{proof}

\section{The bridge: birth and recurrence of a prime}

\begin{theorem}[First appearance and periodic recurrence]\label{thm:bridge}
Let $n \ge 13$ be prime. Then there is a prime $p$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item (\emph{Birth at $n$}) $p \mid F_n$ and $p \nmid F_k$ for all $0 < k < n$;
\item (\emph{Periodic recurrence}) there is $p^\star > 0$ (the order of the
transfer unit modulo $p$) with $F_{n + t p^\star} \equiv 0 \pmod p$ for all
$t \ge 0$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:prim} gives $p$ with (1). Theorem~\ref{thm:period} with $m = p$
gives $p^\star > 0$ with $F_{a+p^\star} \equiv F_a \pmod p$; iterating from
$a = n$ and using $F_n \equiv 0 \pmod p$ gives (2).
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms and complexity perspective}

\textbf{Fast operator powering.} $F_k \bmod m = (M^{k+1})_{1,1}$ is computed by
binary exponentiation of $2\times2$ matrices over $\Z/m\Z$ in $O(\log k)$ matrix
multiplications.

\textbf{Pisano period via operator order.} Multiply by $M$ modulo $m$ until the
running power returns to the identity; the step count is $\pi(m)$.

\textbf{Correlation simulation and order-finding.} A distant value $F_N \bmod m$
is $(M^{N+1})_{1,1}$; collapsing $M^{N+1}$ quickly requires reducing $N$ modulo
the operator's order, and for composite $m$ computing that order is the
order-finding problem---easy quantumly, not known to be easy classically.

\textbf{A one-way index.} For prime indices, $n \mapsto (\text{a primitive prime
divisor of } F_n)$ is easy forward and appears hard to invert, matching the
profile of a one-way function.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

The framework unifies two classical Fibonacci phenomena under one operator. The
periodicity result is structural---it invokes only that a unit of a finite group
has finite order and pinpoints the period as the operator's order---while the
prime case of the primitive-divisor theorem is elementary and self-certifying.

\emph{Future directions.} (1) The period equals the multiplicative order of the
reduced transfer operator, computable in time polynomial in the digit-length of
the modulus given the factorization of the group order. (2) Predicting a distant
correlation value modulo a composite modulus, to within a constant multiplicative
bound on the index, is computationally equivalent to order-finding of the
transfer operator; a polynomial-time predictor would yield a polynomial-time
order-finder. (3) For prime indices, the first-appearance index of a primitive
prime divisor is a one-way function of that divisor.

\end{document}

